From bfdd8ca85638c6945f1dd9ed264e6603b9e843dc Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 4 Aug 2026 17:36:02 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] fix(editor): stop deleting the user's prompt when an AI
 request fails
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Every failure path in the chat panel called onMessagesUpdate(baseMessages) —
the message list from BEFORE the optimistic user message. So the prompt the
user had just written was erased from the thread along with any chance of
resending it, and a five-second toast was the only evidence anything had
happened. That is the "I hit send and nothing happens" report: not a missing
error, a disappearing message.

The paths had also drifted apart. A failure arriving MID-STREAM was written
into the thread with its cause (added after a drained OpenRouter balance 402'd
every build invisibly for days). A failure arriving BEFORE the stream opened —
any non-2xx — got a toast and, in most cases, fell through to a bare
`throw new Error("API error: 500")`. Same failure, opposite treatment,
depending only on whether the response headers had been sent.

And the pre-stream paths never read the server's error body, which is the only
thing distinguishing two very different 402s: "this user is out of app credits"
and "the platform's OpenRouter balance is empty". Both rendered as "add credits
or upgrade your plan". A user acting on that tops up their own account and
watches nothing change, because the problem was never theirs — the same
invisibility, one layer down.

describeAiFailure() is now the single source for all of it, covering the
provider-balance case (recognised by text, since it arrives as 402, as 500, and
mid-stream with no status at all), user credits, the Live lock, rate limits,
auth, payload size and 5xx. Every branch says the message survived, because now
it does. Wired into all four paths: chat, agent, the mid-stream handlers, and
Try-to-fix — which previously always blamed the daily free-fix quota, leaving a
user waiting for a reset that would not help.

Also enables allowImportingTsExtensions. The repo's tests run under
`node --test`, which requires the .ts extension on relative imports, so every
test file reported TS5097 for doing the only thing that works — noise a real
error could hide in. It only requires noEmit, which was already set.

25 tests pass under `node --test`. tsc --noEmit: 51 errors before, 47 after
(the 4 removed are those TS5097s); no new errors. Production build passes.
